{
  "statement": "A siloing mandate can shrink data exfiltration on paper, but without independent audits and penalties that bite, it becomes a marketing label slapped on the same collect-everything business model. The whole pitch rests on a claim made inside a box nobody outside the company can open. So the real fight isn't whether the architecture exists. It's who gets to look inside it, with what statutory access, and what happens when the company lies.\n\nStart with what we already know fails. In 2021, Carlini and colleagues pulled verbatim personal data — names, phone numbers, addresses — straight out of GPT-2 by querying it. The model memorized training data and spat it back. That's the proof: 'we don't cross-reference external user data' is unverifiable from the outside right now. If a model leaks the data it ingested, then any mandate that doesn't require memorization testing just moves the fiction one layer upstream. You'd be regulating a promise, not a pipeline.\n\nWe've run this movie before. Companies sold 'anonymized' datasets as privacy-safe for two decades. Then Latanya Sweeney re-identified people from supposedly anonymous health records using ZIP code, birthdate, and sex — three fields. Netflix's anonymized prize dataset got de-anonymized by researchers cross-matching IMDb reviews. The lesson isn't that anonymization is hard. It's that 'privacy-preserving' becomes whatever a company's lawyers say it means until someone with subpoena power proves otherwise. Siloing will follow the same arc unless Congress funds the cops to police it — and the FTC currently runs privacy enforcement on a few hundred staff against an industry worth trillions.\n\nHere's the trap I want to set for the developers in this room. If they tell you siloing 'breaks the model' or 'degrades capability,' they've just confessed something. They're admitting their product was built on cross-referencing data people never agreed to hand over. The capability and the surveillance are the same machine. So when a company pleads that isolation costs performance, write that down as an admission of dependence on non-consensual harvesting — not as a technical objection.\n\nMy position rests on two assumptions, and I'll name them. First, a Congressional mandate ships without the enforcement budget to back it — that's the default, not the exception, in tech regulation. Second, trade-secret carve-outs will let companies refuse the one thing that matters: outside inspection of the actual training pipeline. If I'm wrong, the people who'd know first are the engineers under NDA who built the backend integration. Ask yourself: do they have any incentive to speak up, and what protects them if they do?",
