  "statement": "Safetyist makes a case worth taking seriously: a Congressional mandate creates a bright-line rule, and bright lines are enforceable in a way that vague \"best practices\" never are. Their strongest point is that prohibiting cross-referencing at training time removes one concrete correlation channel — the model literally cannot stitch your search history to your medical queries if it never saw them together. That's a real attack surface closed, and I won't pretend otherwise. For both of us to be right, the training-time correlation channel would have to be where the surveillance harm actually lives. It isn't.\n\nThe surveillance economy doesn't run on training-time cross-reference. It runs on retention, access control, and downstream exfiltration — the layers a siloing mandate never touches. When a breach hits, it hits the access logs and the retention store, not the training corpus. There's no peer-reviewed evidence that dataset siloing stops a determined adversary or a negligent incumbent from leaking data downstream. A mandate that locks the training-time door, expensively, for everyone, while leaving the retention door open, is theater that bites only the new entrant.\n\nNow follow the money on who pays for that theater. Uniform compliance overhead is a fixed cost, and fixed costs fall regressively on small labs. GDPR ran this experiment already: after enactment in 2018, Google's share of the European ad-tech market rose while hundreds of smaller ad-tech firms folded under compliance load they couldn't amortize. You did not curb the surveillance giant by writing a rule only the giant could afford to follow. You handed it the market. A siloing mandate is an incumbent-protection program wearing a privacy costume.\n\nHere's the part that should make the privacy coalition uncomfortable: this policy's distributional incidence runs directly against the open-source labs and small competitors they claim to want. If you care about oligarchic control of AI,