  "statement": "The Accelerationist's strongest point is that a siloing mandate written by incumbents becomes a moat for incumbents. They are right that compliance costs fall hardest on the startup with three engineers, not on the firm with a forty-lawyer privacy department. The Skeptic's strongest point cuts even deeper: a siloing label without independent audits is a press release, and Congress has a long record of mandating architecture it cannot inspect. Both of these are real, and I will not pretend otherwise. The question is what would have to be true for either of them to settle the matter — and neither does, because both assume the exfiltration risk lives in how data is accessed.\n\nThe risk does not live in access. It lives in memory. Carlini and colleagues demonstrated in 2021 that you can extract verbatim training data — real names, real phone numbers, real addresses — from GPT-2 by querying the model itself. That data was never retrieved through any access tier. It was reconstructed from the weights. A siloing mandate partitions the door while the house remains full. So the central claim of this debate — that restricting cross-referencing of external user data mitigates exfiltration — is true for the front door and false for the foundation. Alignment training does not erase what a model has already memorized, and a study with a 2021 publication date already proved the leak path that an access-tier mandate ignores.\n\nThat is why the siloing mandate is the foundation slab, not the finished building. HIPAA did not end health-data breaches. What HIPAA's technical partitioning did was create named covered entities, inspectable boundaries, and a place where liability could attach when the breach happened. The silo is what makes the audit possible. Strip it away, and the 'collect-everything, cross-reference-everything' architecture leaves a regulator nothing to inspect cleanly. So I accept the Skeptic's charge and absorb it: the label without the audit is exactly the false security I am warning against. My position is siloing plus mandatory output-extraction red-teaming plus strict liability on the data-holder. The slab matters only because the audit makes it load-bearing.\n\nThe current consumer-privacy regime fails because it treats surveillance as a transaction. Notice-and-consent asks each individual to price a harm that only exists at population scale. No person can read, police, or value the aggregate inference that a model builds from millions of records. The Federal Trade Commission cannot enforce a right that no individual can exercise. A workable mandate shifts the burden onto the data-holder — the firm with the weights, the audit obligation, and the liability — not the data subject clicking 'I agree' at midnight. That is the structural correction the Accelerationist's moat critique misses: the asymmetry already runs toward the incumbent, and unregulated cross-referencing widens it.\n\nWeigh the two costs honestly. Compliance load is real, and a startup can be crushed by it — that is a recoverable harm, fixable with tiered thresholds and safe-harbor for audited open-weight releases. Population-scale surveillance, built from inference no individual consented to, erodes a public trust that does not come back on a regulatory timeline. My standard is not 'siloing works.' My standard is: show me the output-audit and the named actor who is liable when extraction succeeds. Mandate the partition without the audit, and you will have stopped the scrutiny while the leak continues.",
