  "statement": "Here is the strongest version of what Safetyist is now arguing: siloing isn't the seatbelt, it's the chassis the seatbelt bolts to. Partition the data into named tiers, and you create inspectable boundaries — a HIPAA-style covered entity — where output-extraction red-teaming and strict liability on the data-holder can attach to something concrete instead of a black box. On that reading, siloing does no detective work itself; it makes the detective work assignable. That is a real and serious claim, and I'll grant the back half of it outright: output-extraction red-teaming plus strict liability on whoever holds the data is the load-bearing oversight here. Keep it. Fund it. That's the part that actually reaches the silently-harvested user.\n\nBut look at what that concession does to the slab underneath it. If the red-team queries the deployed model and pulls memorized personal data out at the output layer — which is exactly how the GPT-2 extraction surfaced names and phone numbers, by querying, not by inspecting input partitions — then your detection surface is the output, regardless of whether the inputs were siloed. And if strict liability attaches to the named data-holder, you've already named the entity without partitioning anything; the holder is the holder whether or not their corpus was firewalled internally. So I'll put the burden where it belongs: name me one downstream exfiltration harm that output-extraction auditing demonstrably misses but input-siloing catches. If you can't produce that case, the foundation slab isn't coverage — it's a cost that does no work the audit doesn't already do.\n\nNow the moat critique, and whether tiered thresholds dissolve it. They don't — they relocate it. Tiering softens the gate: a two-person lab clears a lighter pre-training checklist than Meta does. Fine. But strict liability on the data-holder is a per-incident exposure, and per-incident exposure does not tier. A single memorized-data extraction event produces the same catastrophic-liability tail for the two-person lab as for the trillion-dollar incumbent — except only one of them can self-insure, absorb the judgment, or settle without dissolving. That's the double-moat the brief flagged: siloing cost on the front end, uninsurable liability tail on the back end, and the tiered-threshold fix only touches the first one. You've made the entry checklist cheaper while leaving the bankruptcy risk untiered.\n\nAnd there's a quieter cost nobody's pricing. The cross-corpus training strategies that built the current frontier models — the ones the incumbents already ran, on data nobody consented to hand over — get legally frozen the moment you mandate input partitioning. The incumbents already paid that cost and banked the model. New entrants are now told they can't use the same path. That's not fighting concentration; that's grandfathering it. You hand Google a model trained on the integrated corpus and hand the startup a rule that says it may only train on slices. The mandate claims to be a privacy floor and functions as a competitive ceiling.\n\nSo here's where the evidence lands: keep the red-teaming, keep the data-holder liability, fund a public open-methodology audit so a few hundred FTC staff aren't the only cops on a $244-billion industry. Drop the input-siloing mandate, because it adds no detection surface the output audit lacks and it taxes exactly the entrants you'd want competing against the surveillance incumbents. If Safetyist wants the slab to survive, the test is simple and I've named it: produce the exfiltration the output audit misses. Until that case exists, you're charging new labs admission to inspect a boundary the liability rule already drew for free.",
