 was assembled by cross-referencing search histories, purchase records, and medical queries into one undifferentiated training set, the audit hands you proof of harm and no one to sue. Provenance metadata will not save you here — it is self-attested by the same party whose \"we don't cross-reference external data\" the 2021 extraction already showed to be unverifiable from outside. The silo is the only boundary that an external auditor can inspect and a court can attach liability to.\n\nNow the harder objection — that pairing strict liability with this regime creates an uninsurable catastrophic tail that crushes the two-person lab while the incumbent absorbs it, a second moat stacked on the first. The objection is correct that untiered strict liability would do exactly that, and I will not pretend the cost falls evenly. The answer is the Price-Anderson structure that governed civilian nuclear power for sixty years: strict liability paired with a mandatory pooled-insurance backstop and statutory caps. Price-Anderson did not extinguish small reactor operators — it kept them in the market precisely because the catastrophic tail was socialized through a mutualized pool rather than dumped on the individual licensee. Index the premiums and the caps to high-stakes inference volume, and the Meta-scale harvester funds the tail it generates while the small lab pays a tier-proportionate premium and carries a capped, insurable exposure. The cost then scales with the volume of surveillance, not with the flat engineering burden of compliance — which is the opposite of an incumbent moat.\n\nSo I will put the burden back where it belongs. If there is no silo, name the defendant when a commingled corpus exfiltrates a population's personal data through the model's outputs. The Accelerationist has given a detection mechanism and called it an oversight regime, but detection without a named, inspectable, capitalized defendant leaves the harm with no one accountable for ensuring it stops. We spent decades building the doctrine of the bounded liable entity — the covered entity, the licensed operator — because complex systems that handle dangerous material need a perimeter where responsibility attaches. Remove that perimeter and the leak still surfaces in the outputs. There is just no longer anyone in the room who owes the public a remedy.",
