ce, and those are different instruments. The output audit answers 'did data leak.' The commitment answers 'did the provider lie about the corpus that leaked.' One is forensics; the other is perjury with a timestamp.\n\nThe Skeptic's sharpest point is that verifying access tiers across every frontier provider needs a standing technical audit corps Congress has not appropriated and neither opponent has costed. I grant the human-corps cost is real and that I have not costed it. The escape is that cryptographic commitment verification does not scale with human inspectors — it scales with compute, the way Certificate Transparency verifies TLS certificates across the entire web with no per-site auditor. When DigiNotar fraudulently issued certificates for Google domains in 2011, the fraud was caught through publicly auditable append-only logs, not an appropriated inspector corps. I will mark the boundary of my own analogy before the Skeptic does: Certificate Transparency verifies a narrow cryptographic fact — that a certificate was issued — far simpler than 'no cross-referencing occurred inside a trillion-parameter run.' The commitment regime I am defending verifies the partition boundary the provider swore to, not the full training process. That is the honest ceiling of the claim, and it is still a pre-harm object where today there is none.\n\nTurn this on the Accelerationist's model — output red-teaming plus strict liability on the deployer — at the joint my own prior argument left exposed. Strict liability answers 'who pays.' It does not answer 'what did they promise.' A Meta-scale harvester budgets a Meta-scale judgment as a cost of doing business, settles, and assembles the next corpus identically, because nothing in the regime made the second corpus a documented violation of a sworn boundary. Liability without an ex-ante commitment is a toll the surveillance incumbent prices into the quarter. The commitment is what converts the toll into a deterrent — and the population's data is already in the wild by the time the check clears, which is the entire reason the verification has to bind before the run, not after the breach.",
