n input mandate, the manifest is the regulated object, so a firm that lies on it buys safety — it satisfies the letter and omits the join. Under output-conditioned liability, the manifest stops being a shield. When the commingled corpus exfiltrates a population's personal data through the model's outputs, the clean manifest that omitted the join step is no longer a compliance document. It is documentary evidence that the provider represented partitioned sourcing while operating a commingled pipeline — knowing misrepresentation, which escalates the firm from strict liability into fraud. The lie that bought everything under the input regime now buys a worse defendant posture. The manifest becomes the noose, not the alibi.\n\nThe Skeptic's actuarial point is the load-bearing beam I will adopt rather than resist: a mutualized insurance pool runs on underwriters who must price the catastrophic tail, and pricing the tail forces the licensee to demonstrate audit-readiness continuously, not produce documents after the leak surfaces. That continuous readiness is the standing instrumentation an input mandate could never compel — but it is privately ordered and self-funded, paid by the insured industry as a premium, not stood up as a permanent federal corps with the few-hundred-staff FTC that cannot police a trillion-dollar sector. Nuclear underwriters demand site inspection before they bind coverage on a reactor; the Price-Anderson pool kept small operators solvent precisely by mutualizing the tail rather than dumping it on one licensee. The output-extraction audit supplies the loss trigger; the insurer's pricing demand supplies the ex-ante pressure. Neither requires Congress to fund an inspectorate to regenerate a join graph that cannot be regenerated.\n\nI will not overstate what this regime does. The Skeptic is right that liability works after exfiltration, when some of the population's data is already in the wild — the prevention-versus-pricing gap is real, and an actuarial premium narrows it without closing it. But a static input partition closes nothing: it is unverifiable on the day it is enacted, and an unverifiable rule deters only firms that were never going to commingle in the first place. The choice Congress actually faces is between a partition mandate that names no enforceable surface and a liability regime that prices the one surface an outsider can independently confirm. Write the statute against the boundary no auditor can inspect, and you have passed a press release. Write it against the leak the audit can demonstrate, and you have named a defendant before the next corpus reaches the wild.",
