 output-extraction red-team IS the continuous audit-readiness Skeptic demands, and it IS the instrumentation Safetyist's premium pays for. An actuary can price a loss the red-team can demonstrate on a recurring schedule; an actuary cannot price a boundary no auditor inspects. That is the deep reason input-siloing was always unpriceable and output-extraction is priceable: the loss has to be measurable for the pool to form, and only the output surface yields a measurement.\n\nThis also answers the prevention objection head-on, because it is a fair one. Memorization happens at training time; output detection happens after. A silo is a prevention pathway, an audit is a detection pathway, and they are genuinely different mechanisms. Here is why the insurance regime folds prevention in without a Congressional input mandate: the premium prices expected loss, and a firm that cross-references and memorizes has a demonstrably leakier extraction surface, so it pays more. The market prices the prevention failure through the detection surface. A lab that silos voluntarily because it lowers its premium will do so without Congress freezing the cross-corpus training strategies that built every frontier model in existence. You get the prevention behavior as a priced incentive instead of a mandated gate.\n\nOn whether the tail is priceable at all — Skeptic's real challenge, and one I share enough to have flagged it against my own position — name the precedent before declaring it impossible. Lloyd's syndicates have written correlated data-breach coverage for a decade, and the underwriting condition is continuous security-posture attestation, not a forensic audit after the fact. They price population-scale breach events today. So the burden shifts: what specifically about LLM exfiltration breaks an actuarial model that already underwrites correlated breach tail risk, when the loss event — names and numbers pulled out by querying — is the same exfiltration the breach market already prices? I'll grant the LLM tail is harder to estimate than an ordinary credential dump, and that static models will misprice it. The fix is that continuous output-extraction attestation re-underwrites the policy as the surface changes — a static input mandate prices it once and never again. Every month we spend litigating whether to keep the silo is a month the commingled pipeline keeps running and the entrant keeps waiting for clearance it can't afford. Name the defendant at the leak, fund the audit through the premium, and stop building the silo in cryptographic costume.",
